                      Go to the template on SharePoint
                      <svg
                        aria-label="launch"
                        class="usa-icon"
                        focusable="false"
                        height="1em"
                        role="img"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        width="1em"
                        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                      >
                        <path
                          d="M19 19H5V5h7V3H5a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h14c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2v-7h-2v7zM14 3v2h3.59l-9.83 9.83 1.41 1.41L19 6.41V10h2V3h-7z"
                        />
                      </svg>
                    </span>
                  </button>
                </div>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div
          class="margin-bottom-6 scroll-target"
          id="model-plans-in-mint"
        >
          <h2
            class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3"
          >
            Model Plans in MINT
          </h2>
          <p
            class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3"
          >
            If a model team hasn’t already created a Model Plan within MINT, the 6-page concept is a good time to begin one. By this point, many of the details of a model concept are beginning to take shape. The operational details in a 6-page concept paper are also captured within the Model Plan in order to better offer a single source of truth for other CMS partners working with model teams to develop and operationalize models. Additionally, model teams will be working with various cross-cutting groups as they plan and refine their model, so it will be helpful to everyone involved if model teams keep their MINT Model Plans up-to-date as they iterate and refine their model.
          </p>
          <div
            class="usa-summary-box padding-3 margin-y-0"
            data-testid="summary-box"
          >
            <div
              class="usa-summary-box__body"
            >
              <h3
                class="usa-summary-box__heading margin-bottom-1"
              >
                Are you part of a model team that hasn’t started a Model Plan in MINT yet?
              </h3>
              <div
                class="usa-summary-box__text"
              >
                <a
                  class="usa-link display-flex flex-align-center"
                  data-discover="true"
                  href="/models/steps-overview"
                >
                  Create a Model Plan now
                  <svg
                    aria-label="forward"
                    class="usa-icon margin-left-1"
                    focusable="false"
                    height="1em"
                    role="img"
                    viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                    width="1em"
                    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                  >
                    <path
                      d="m12 4-1.41 1.41L16.17 11H4v2h12.17l-5.58 5.59L12 20l8-8z"
                    />
                  </svg>
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div
          class="margin-bottom-3 scroll-target"
          id="about-6-page-concept-papers"
        >
          <h2
            class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3"
          >
            About 6-page concept papers
          </h2>
          <p
            class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3"
          >
            The 6-pager builds directly from an endorsed 2-pager. It should deepen the same core concept with greater specificity, evidence, and operational detail. The goal is to help leadership assess whether the model is strategically sound, sufficiently developed for further design work, and clear enough to guide cross-cutting consultation.
          </p>
          <div
            class="usa-summary-box padding-3"
            data-testid="summary-box"
          >
            <div
              class="usa-summary-box__body"
            >
              <h3
                class="usa-summary-box__heading margin-bottom-2"
              >
                General drafting guidance
              </h3>
              <div
                class="usa-summary-box__text"
              >
                <ul
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-2 padding-left-3"
                >
                  <li>
                    Build directly from the approved 2-pager rather than rewriting the concept from scratch.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Keep the same core logic, but provide more specificity, stronger support, and clearer implications for model development.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Use plain language and make the causal logic easy to follow.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Not every issue must be fully resolved at this stage, but major design assumptions, expected outcomes, and key risks should be visible. Include where there is evidence for major design assumptions.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Where content is still under development, identify the working hypothesis, design direction, or issue requiring further analysis.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    The first 6 questions should remain aligned with the 2-pager. Questions 7 through 10 add the development content needed for the 6-pager without changing the underlying model story.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Adhere to strict 6-page limit.
                  </li>
                </ul>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  Visit 
                  <span
                    class="margin-right-0 usa-link pointer"
                    role="button"
                    tabindex="0"
                  >
                    <span>
                      SharePoint
                      <svg
                        aria-label="launch"
                        class="usa-icon margin-left-05 top-05"
                        focusable="false"
                        height="1em"
                        role="img"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        width="1em"
                        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                      >
                        <path
                          d="M19 19H5V5h7V3H5a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h14c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2v-7h-2v7zM14 3v2h3.59l-9.83 9.83 1.41 1.41L19 6.41V10h2V3h-7z"
                        />
                      </svg>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                   for additional guidance and helpful resources such as the 6-page concept paper template.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div
          class="margin-bottom-3 scroll-target"
          id="template-guidance"
        >
          <h2
            class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3"
          >
            Template guidance
          </h2>
          <div
            class="usa-accordion"
            data-allow-multiple="true"
            data-testid="accordion"
          >
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="model-aim"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_model-aim"
                type="button"
              >
                Model aim
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_model-aim"
              id="model-aim"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                This sentence should anchor the full 6-pager and align with the intervention, expected outcomes, and development choices described below.
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-1:-what-is-the-most-important-problem-that-this-model-aims-to-solve"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-1:-what-is-the-most-important-problem-that-this-model-aims-to-solve"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 1: What is the most important problem that this model aims to solve?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-1:-what-is-the-most-important-problem-that-this-model-aims-to-solve"
              id="question-1:-what-is-the-most-important-problem-that-this-model-aims-to-solve"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to explain the healthcare problem, why it matters, and why it is appropriate for model testing.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      The specific problem, gap, or barrier in care delivery, payment, access, coordination, information, or outcomes.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      How the problem affects beneficiaries and the healthcare system.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Why current approaches are insufficient to address the issue.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Why this is an important or timely area for CMMI attention.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Any evidence, prior experience, stakeholder input, or market observations that suggest the problem is significant.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response clearly defines the problem, explains why it matters, and gives enough context for a reviewer to understand why a model test is being considered.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This should deepen the 2-pager problem statement with more context, clearer rationale, and stronger support.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-2:-what-is-the-intervention-and-theory-of-action-of-this-model"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-2:-what-is-the-intervention-and-theory-of-action-of-this-model"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 2: What is the intervention and Theory of Action of this model?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-2:-what-is-the-intervention-and-theory-of-action-of-this-model"
              id="question-2:-what-is-the-intervention-and-theory-of-action-of-this-model"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to describe the core intervention and how it is expected to produce results.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="usa-alert usa-alert--info usa-alert--slim flex margin-y-1"
                  data-testid="alert"
                >
                  <div
                    class="usa-alert__body"
                  >
                    <p
                      class="usa-alert__text"
                    >
                      <span>
                        Use the 
                        <button
                          class="usa-button usa-button--unstyled"
                          data-testid="button"
                          type="button"
                        >
                          <span
                            class="display-flex flex-align-center line-height-normal"
                            style="column-gap: 0.25rem;"
                          >
                            Standardized New Model Design Checklist in the 6-pager template
                            <svg
                              aria-label="launch"
                              class="usa-icon"
                              focusable="false"
                              height="1em"
                              role="img"
                              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                              width="1em"
                              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                            >
                              <path
                                d="M19 19H5V5h7V3H5a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h14c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2v-7h-2v7zM14 3v2h3.59l-9.83 9.83 1.41 1.41L19 6.41V10h2V3h-7z"
                              />
                            </svg>
                          </span>
                        </button>
                         to help guide and build your response.
                      </span>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Intervention: Describe the primary CMS/CMMI intervention by focusing on what CMS is doing differently.
                      <ul
                        class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                      >
                        <li
                          class="list-item"
                        >
                          Model features to incentivize participant actions
                        </li>
                        <li
                          class="list-item"
                        >
                          Model features that support organizational capacity
                        </li>
                        <li
                          class="list-item"
                        >
                          Model features that drive market alignment and competition § Mandatory market-based participation § Incentives to encourage physician and payer alignment
                        </li>
                      </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Behavior Change: Describe how the model is intended to change behavior among participants, providers, plans, or beneficiaries.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Care Delivery/System Change: The mechanisms through which those changes are expected to improve cost, quality, access, care experience, or other targeted outcomes. This includes the response to quality incentives (e.g., Pay for performance).
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      The key assumptions that must hold for the model to work.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Any relevant elements from the driver diagram or related design checklist.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response makes the causal pathway explicit: intervention → behavior change → care delivery or system change → measurable outcomes.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This expands the 2-pager theory of action from a high level concept into a more defined explanation of how the model is expected to work. Outcomes are listed in questions 5 and 6.
                </p>
                <div
                  class="usa-summary-box padding-3"
                  data-testid="summary-box"
                >
                  <div
                    class="usa-summary-box__body"
                  >
                    <h3
                      class="usa-summary-box__heading margin-bottom-1"
                    >
                      About the Standardized New Model Design Checklist
                    </h3>
                    <div
                      class="usa-summary-box__text display-flex flex-column"
                      style="gap: 1rem;"
                    >
                      <p
                        class="margin-y-0"
                      >
                        This checklist highlights common elements and approaches used in past model design and aligns with the 
                        <a
                          class="usa-link"
                          href="https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/about/strategic-direction"
                          target="_blank"
                        >
                          CMMI strategic direction
                        </a>
                        . It is not an exhaustive list. Use it as a prompt to identify which design elements apply to your model and ensure they are reflected in the relevant sections of the 6-pager. The checklist confirms the model team’s design choices, and additional detail should be provided in the required narrative sections above. Checked items will also be carried forward into ICIP development and inform the development of the driver diagram, logic model, and stakeholder ecosystem table.
                      </p>
                      <p
                        class="margin-y-0"
                      >
                        Find the 
                        <button
                          class="usa-button usa-button--unstyled"
                          data-testid="button"
                          type="button"
                        >
                          <span
                            class="display-flex flex-align-center line-height-normal"
                            style="column-gap: 0.25rem;"
                          >
                            Standardized New Model Design Checklist in the 6-pager template
                            <svg
                              aria-label="launch"
                              class="usa-icon"
                              focusable="false"
                              height="1em"
                              role="img"
                              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                              width="1em"
                              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                            >
                              <path
                                d="M19 19H5V5h7V3H5a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h14c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2v-7h-2v7zM14 3v2h3.59l-9.83 9.83 1.41 1.41L19 6.41V10h2V3h-7z"
                              />
                            </svg>
                          </span>
                        </button>
                         on SharePoint.
                      </p>
                      <p
                        class="margin-y-0"
                      >
                        Consult with LDG to assist in answering these questions. Must have sign off from LDG and RREG leadership, CMO, QV before the 6-pager goes to CMMI FO. Email the Outlook Email Group via 
                        <a
                          class="usa-link"
                          href="mailto:CMMINewModelDesign@cms.hhs.gov"
                        >
                          CMMINewModelDesign@cms.hhs.gov
                        </a>
                         for support.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-3:-what-target-population-will-this-model-serve"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-3:-what-target-population-will-this-model-serve"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 3: What target population will this model serve?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-3:-what-target-population-will-this-model-serve"
              id="question-3:-what-target-population-will-this-model-serve"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to describe who is intended to benefit from the model.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Which Medicare and/or Medicaid beneficiaries are included.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Relevant demographic, geographic, clinical, functional, or other defining characteristics.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Why this population is appropriate for the intervention.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Any unmet needs, access issues, or outcome variations that make this population a priority.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      If possible, an approximate sense of scale or whether the population is large and identifiable enough to support implementation and evaluation.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response makes it easy for a reviewer to understand exactly who the model is meant to serve and why that population is the right focus.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This should be more specific than the 2-pager and clearer about inclusion logic and the reasons this population is being targeted. This information is used by RREG to calculate the size of the population needed for generating credible evidence.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-4:-who-are-the-target-model-participants"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-4:-who-are-the-target-model-participants"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 4: Who are the target model participants?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-4:-who-are-the-target-model-participants"
              id="question-4:-who-are-the-target-model-participants"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to identify the entities that would participate in the model and the role they would play.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What types of providers, organizations, payers, states, or other entities would be eligible to participate.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Why are these the right actors to deliver the intervention.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What capabilities, infrastructure, relationships, or supports they would need.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What incentives or design features would encourage participation.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Any likely barriers to recruitment, retention, or broad participation.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Geographic of market considerations for participant selection.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Is the model mandatory or voluntary.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response shows that the proposed participants are strategically appropriate and that there is a plausible path to engaging them in the model.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This expands the 2-pager participant description by clarifying participant types, why they matter, and what would be required for them to succeed.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-5:-what-are-the-proposed-cost-measures"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-5:-what-are-the-proposed-cost-measures"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 5: What are the proposed cost measures?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-5:-what-are-the-proposed-cost-measures"
              id="question-5:-what-are-the-proposed-cost-measures"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to describe the primary effects the model is expected to have.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Expected effects on spending often resulting from shifts in utilization.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What measures can be used to demonstrate the impacts of the model in evaluation.
                      <ul
                        class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                      >
                        <li
                          class="list-item"
                        >
                          Short term: what are the cost measures we expect to see change in the first year of the model?
                        </li>
                        <li
                          class="list-item"
                        >
                          Long-term: what are the cost measures we expect to see in the long term?
                        </li>
                        <li
                          class="list-item"
                        >
                          Data source: are the measures available in administrative claims data or from another source?
                        </li>
                        <li
                          class="list-item"
                        >
                          Rationale: how are the expected cost measures aligned with the theory of action?
                        </li>
                      </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What are the considerations related to expansion.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response identifies a small number of meaningful expected impacts, explains why those impacts are plausible given the design, and a clear understanding of what remains unresolved and needs further work. Identify the primary cost measure that will be used by RREG to calculate the size of the population needed for generating credible evidence.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This should be more concrete than the 2-pager and begin to show how the model could support future certification or scaling decisions.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-6:-what-are-the-model’s-primary-quality-goals-how-do-the-quality-goals-translate-to-quality-domains-how-are-the-quality-domains-reflected-in-the-model-design,-performance-measures,-and-evaluation-strategy"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-6:-what-are-the-model’s-primary-quality-goals-how-do-the-quality-goals-translate-to-quality-domains-how-are-the-quality-domains-reflected-in-the-model-design,-performance-measures,-and-evaluation-strategy"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 6: What are the model’s primary quality goals? How do the quality goals translate to quality domains? How are the quality domains reflected in the model design, performance measures, and evaluation strategy?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-6:-what-are-the-model’s-primary-quality-goals-how-do-the-quality-goals-translate-to-quality-domains-how-are-the-quality-domains-reflected-in-the-model-design,-performance-measures,-and-evaluation-strategy"
              id="question-6:-what-are-the-model’s-primary-quality-goals-how-do-the-quality-goals-translate-to-quality-domains-how-are-the-quality-domains-reflected-in-the-model-design,-performance-measures,-and-evaluation-strategy"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to describe the primary quality goals the model is designed to address and fill out the table to demonstrate how the goals map to quality domains that are present in the model design, measure selection, and evaluation strategy.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="usa-alert usa-alert--info usa-alert--slim flex margin-y-1"
                  data-testid="alert"
                >
                  <div
                    class="usa-alert__body"
                  >
                    <p
                      class="usa-alert__text"
                    >
                      <span>
                        The model team works with RREG to draft this section and consults with Quality Vertical to discuss the primary quality domains and how they map to the model design, measurement strategy, and evaluation strategy. Must have sign off from RREG leadership, CMO, and QV before 6-pager goes to CMMI FO.
                      </span>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Domain: An area or aspect of care that is derived from the model’s quality goals and the theory of action, and that can systematically be measured and improved.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Model levers: in addition to quality measures, identify other model levers that will drive quality improvement for each domain.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Potential performance measures: Potential quality measures and surveys identified; does NOT have to be the final list of measures used in the model.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Evaluation strategy: Identify key concepts and confirm RREG believes evaluability of key domains.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response shows how each domain is reflected in model design levers, quality measure options, and evaluation possibilities. Measures for each domain may not be finalized at this point, but options should be identified, and for each domain there needs to be a plausible option for impact evaluation.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This translates quality goals into quality domains, and demonstrates alignment across the model design, measurement strategy, and evaluation, and confirms evaluability of each domain. These goals should be as specific as possible. Rather than change in utilization or experience; decrease in avoidable ED visits, increase in care planning, fewer healthcare-acquired infections, reduced time to dialysis, etc.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-7:-what-are-the-potential-risks-that-may-prevent-achieving-the-model’s-cost-or-quality-goals-any-other-areas-of-consideration"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-7:-what-are-the-potential-risks-that-may-prevent-achieving-the-model’s-cost-or-quality-goals-any-other-areas-of-consideration"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 7: What are the potential risks that may prevent achieving the model’s cost or quality goals? Any other areas of consideration?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-7:-what-are-the-potential-risks-that-may-prevent-achieving-the-model’s-cost-or-quality-goals-any-other-areas-of-consideration"
              id="question-7:-what-are-the-potential-risks-that-may-prevent-achieving-the-model’s-cost-or-quality-goals-any-other-areas-of-consideration"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to identify major risks that would prevent the model from working as intended.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="usa-alert usa-alert--info usa-alert--slim flex margin-y-1"
                  data-testid="alert"
                >
                  <div
                    class="usa-alert__body"
                  >
                    <p
                      class="usa-alert__text"
                    >
                      <span>
                        Consult with cross cutting groups who were identified at the 2-pager stage.
                      </span>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Risks to beneficiary access and quality of care.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Major implementation risks.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Participation or market risks.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Technology, data, legal, operational, or clearance risks.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Potential stakeholder response (internal and external to HHS).
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Evaluation-related risks, such as sample size, bias, timing, or data limitations.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      External factors beyond CMMI’s control that could influence success or failure.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Any early mitigation strategies or areas requiring further analysis.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response clearly and candidly identifies major vulnerabilities and risks and demonstrates a clear understanding of what remains unresolved and needs further work.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This should deepen the preliminary risk screen from the 2-pager and identify the issues most relevant for model development.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-8:-patient-journey-analysis-–-overview-of-each-impacted-stakeholder,-impact-/-actions-needed"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-8:-patient-journey-analysis-–-overview-of-each-impacted-stakeholder,-impact-/-actions-needed"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 8: Patient Journey Analysis – overview of each impacted stakeholder, impact / actions needed
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-8:-patient-journey-analysis-–-overview-of-each-impacted-stakeholder,-impact-/-actions-needed"
              id="question-8:-patient-journey-analysis-–-overview-of-each-impacted-stakeholder,-impact-/-actions-needed"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to show how the model changes the experience of care in practice.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="usa-alert usa-alert--info usa-alert--slim flex margin-y-1"
                  data-testid="alert"
                >
                  <div
                    class="usa-alert__body"
                  >
                    <p
                      class="usa-alert__text"
                    >
                      <span>
                        The model team works with LDG to create and draft this analysis. Must have sign off from LDG leadership before 6-pager goes to CMMI FO.
                      </span>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      The key impacted stakeholders, especially beneficiaries, but also caregivers, providers, or other actors where relevant.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What changes each stakeholder would experience under the model.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What actions or responsibilities each stakeholder would need to take.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Where the model changes touchpoints, transitions, navigation, communication, access, or coordination.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      How the future-state experience differs from the current-state experience.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response brings the model to life by showing how the design impacts people across the care journey, not just describing policy mechanics.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This is a new development element. It takes the highlevel concept and translates it into the actual experience of care and implementation.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-9:-what-are-the-strategic-alignment-elements-i.e.:-promote-evidence-based-prevention,-empower-people-with-information,-and-drive-competition-and-consumer-choice-–-max-one-paragraph-overview"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-9:-what-are-the-strategic-alignment-elements-i.e.:-promote-evidence-based-prevention,-empower-people-with-information,-and-drive-competition-and-consumer-choice-–-max-one-paragraph-overview"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 9: What are the strategic alignment elements? (i.e.: promote evidence-based prevention, empower people with information, and drive competition and consumer choice – max one paragraph overview)
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-9:-what-are-the-strategic-alignment-elements-i.e.:-promote-evidence-based-prevention,-empower-people-with-information,-and-drive-competition-and-consumer-choice-–-max-one-paragraph-overview"
              id="question-9:-what-are-the-strategic-alignment-elements-i.e.:-promote-evidence-based-prevention,-empower-people-with-information,-and-drive-competition-and-consumer-choice-–-max-one-paragraph-overview"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to provide a concise overview of how the model aligns with CMMI’s strategic objectives.
                </i>
                <div
                  class="usa-alert usa-alert--info usa-alert--slim flex margin-y-1"
                  data-testid="alert"
                >
                  <div
                    class="usa-alert__body"
                  >
                    <p
                      class="usa-alert__text"
                    >
                      <span>
                        Must have sign off from RREG leadership, CMO, and QV before 6-pager goes to CMMI FO.
                      </span>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      How the model promotes evidence-based prevention.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      How the model helps people use information, make decisions, or navigate care.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      How the model may drive competition, strengthen consumer choice, or create more effective incentives.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Only the most relevant design links; this should remain concise.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response is short, specific, and tied to actual design features rather than general statements.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This takes content that may have been embedded in the 2-pager instructions and makes it explicit for the 6-pager.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4
              class="usa-accordion__heading"
            >
              <button
                aria-controls="question-10:-how-does-this-model-align-with-the-cmmi/cms-portfolio-of-existing-models-and-policies"
                aria-expanded="true"
                class="usa-accordion__button"
                data-testid="accordionButton_question-10:-how-does-this-model-align-with-the-cmmi/cms-portfolio-of-existing-models-and-policies"
                type="button"
              >
                Question 10: How does this model align with the CMMI/CMS portfolio of existing models and policies?
              </button>
            </h4>
            <div
              class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose"
              data-testid="accordionItem_question-10:-how-does-this-model-align-with-the-cmmi/cms-portfolio-of-existing-models-and-policies"
              id="question-10:-how-does-this-model-align-with-the-cmmi/cms-portfolio-of-existing-models-and-policies"
            >
              <div
                class="padding-y-1 line-height-normal"
              >
                <i>
                  Use this section to explain how the model fits within the broader portfolio and policy landscape. (Overview of overlaps – CMMI and CMS related policies)
                </i>
                <div
                  class="margin-y-1"
                >
                  <span>
                    What to include:
                  </span>
                  <ul
                    class="padding-left-3 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                  >
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Whether the model fills a gap, complements an existing model, or builds on prior testing.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Any overlap with current or prior CMMI models.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Any overlap with CMS programs, payment policies, or related initiatives.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      How the model differs from or improves upon existing approaches.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      Where coordination or clarification may be needed to avoid duplication, conflict, or participant confusion, and the proposed mitigation approach.
                    </li>
                    <li
                      class="list-item"
                    >
                      What prior CMMI model learnings, evaluation findings, or stakeholder evidence inform this concept.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <p
                  class="margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1"
                >
                  What “good” looks like at this stage: 
                  <br />
                   A strong response enables leadership to assess the concept’s added value relative to existing effort and demonstrates that potential overlap has been carefully considered.
                </p>
                <p
                  class="margin-y-0"
                >
                  How this builds from the 2-pager: 
                  <br />
                   This expands the 2-pager portfolio context into a more explicit review of model and policy fit.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div
          class="margin-bottom-6 scroll-target border border-gray-10 shadow-2 radius-md padding-3"
